// The solution to the second exercise of the platforms chapter: a disabled
// branch may call a function that exists nowhere.
//
// The `cfg(unix)` block is removed on Windows before the compiler resolves a
// single name, so `a_function_that_exists_nowhere` is never looked up. The
// program compiles on every platform, which is the whole point of `cfg`.

fn main() {
    cfg(unix) {
        a_function_that_exists_nowhere();
    }
    print("compiled");
}
